I agree that it is a poor starting place to learn the basics of
programming.  I disagree that it makes a poor example of how good
programs result from group efforts.  Look at the source to almost
any other large group project and it turns out Nethack across its
versions is a good lesson in how to make things work well, in how
to abandon parts that are no longer maintainable and redesign
them, and in how to get a group to work together.  Not a topic
for a beginner, but most excellent for upper division college
junior/senior level courses.

> >I am making the assumption that it is in C++, which is what I am
> >currently learning.
